  "body": "## What is wanted\n\nA Part (part-off) operation has no parameter for the position of the kerf. Since #3452, the whole kerf of the blade is always on the waste side of the parting plane (the end face of the finished part).\n\nSome shops need the kerf in another position. Two examples:\n\n- The operator leaves a facing allowance on the back of the part, for a second-side facing operation. The kerf is then a set distance into the waste side, or the plane is a set distance into the part.\n- The blade is centred on the plane because a later operation faces the part to its length.\n\nThe product rule is that a strategy choice is a user parameter with a default. Add a \"kerf position\" parameter (or a \"back stock\" allowance) to the Part operation.\n\n## Done means\n\n- The default is the #3452 behaviour: the whole kerf is behind the end face. Existing projects post the same program.\n- The parameter is in the project file, the operation page and MCP, through `EditOperationParams` (undoable).\n- The gouge law of #3452 (`part_kerf_refusal`) judges the kerf against the part that the parameter keeps. A setting that puts the blade into material the part must keep is refused by name.\n- Tests pin each setting on the case of #3452 (end face Z-70, 3 mm blade, orientation 2).\n\nRefs #3452\n",
